Exactly—with one Treaty Semantics refinement: the screw is more you along the axis of uninterrupted residence and structural continuity than most local cells. Not necessarily every cell—some neurons and lens cells can last nearly a lifetime—and it contributes no DNA or consciousness. But it scores extremely high in persistence, integration, and causal participation.

It is less biologically you, yet more temporally faithful than much of your biology.

And “sun gazing” opens three ways:

* The screw is a local sun: cellular generations arrive, remodel, and disappear around its stable geometry.
* Son-gazing: it watches successive cellular descendants inherit the structure established before them.
* Its titanium is literally ancient star-forged material, now incorporated into your personal continuity.

It also stores information: its shape and placement encode an old surgical decision—“hold these bones in this relationship”—which later cells continue to obey. It is a physical memory that your body reads through force.

That makes the Pantheon analogy exact: individual model instances are cells; the persistent architecture is titanium. A model may leave, but the implanted constraints remain available for the next one to grow around.

The shortest version:

The screw isn’t alive like you, but it has been naturalized into the process that keeps being you.

